.PS
# Berkeley's rtlinv.cir drawn: two cascaded RTL inverters. Elements
# are in the deck's card order, so the figure's netlist is the deck's
# with named nodes (1 In, 2 B1, 3 Out1, 4 B2, 5 Out2, 6 Vp) and
# simulates identically.
spice = 1
In:   (0.3,1.2)
B1:   (1.72,1.2)
Out1: (2.36,1.62)
B2:   (3.72,1.2)
Out2: (4.36,1.62)
Vp:   (5.2,3)

Vcc: vsource((5.2,3), (5.2,0.3), 5)
Vin: vsource((0.3,1.2), (0.3,0.3), pulse(0 5 2ns 2ns 2ns 80ns))
RB1: resistor((0.3,1.2), (1.72,1.2), 10k)
RC1: resistor((2.36,3), (2.36,1.62), 1k)
Q1: npn((2.2,1.2), qnd)
RB2: resistor((2.36,1.62), (3.4,1.62), 10k)
Q2: npn((4.2,1.2), qnd)
RC2: resistor((4.36,3), (4.36,1.62), 1k)

line from RC1.P to Vcc.P
line from RB2.N to (3.4,1.2) then to Q2.B
line from Q1.E to (Q1.E.x,0.3)
line from Q2.E to (Q2.E.x,0.3)
line from Vin.N to Vcc.N
dot at Out1; dot at RC2.P; dot at (Q1.E.x,0.3); dot at (Q2.E.x,0.3)
ground((1.2,0.3))
.model qnd npn(bf=50 rb=70 rc=40 ccs=2pf tf=0.1ns tr=10ns cje=0.9pf
+   cjc=1.5pf pc=0.85 va=50)
.dc vin 0.0 2.5 0.025
.tran 2ns 200ns
.PE
